  "body": "Currently both the sessionStorage and localStorage do not have an expiry time, like cookies have. I'd like to open a discussion on this.\r\n\r\nNot having an expiration time might result, in very specific scenario's, in storage that becomes available to other users. For instance, on a shared internet-cafe machine, where on a website storage is used by a webmail-provider to speed up (cache) items. The webmail-provider now might have implemented to remove that storage on logout and have implemented a cookie-session timeout of like 15 minutes, but sessionStorage and localStorage might survive that limit if someone just browses away and forgets about the webmail at all.\r\n\r\nOf course there are ways to work around this, like encrypting the sessionStorage and localStorage and putting the encryption key in a cookie that has a timeout. Though I think it's better to fix this issue at the core, and provide sessionStorage and localStorage with an expiry time or provide a core-ability to encrypt it and link it to sometime that does expire.",
